C3 — Inefficient Iteration Patterns


Rule ID
Root cause / pattern
Detection condition
Recommended optimization strategy
Preconditions / constraints
Expected benefit
reference
C3.S1.R1
Index-based iteration
Loop uses range(len(collection)) only to retrieve each element.
Replace with direct element iteration.
Index is not required; element order and side effects remain unchanged.
Reduce unnecessary indexing and access overhead.
[4], [6]
C3.S1.R2


Repeated list appending


A loop builds a list by appending a simple expression for every element.


Replace the loop with a list comprehension, e.g., [x*x for x in numbers].


Preserve ordering, side effects, and exception behavior.


May reduce interpreter overhead and simplify list construction.


[3]
C3.S1.R3
Redundant dictionary key access
.keys() is used only to iterate over dictionary keys.
Iterate directly over the dictionary.
Only keys are required; mutation behavior remains valid.
Avoid an unnecessary method call.
[4]
C3.S1.R4
Inefficient reduction
A custom loop calculates a standard sum, minimum, or maximum.
Use a suitable built-in operation.
The built-in must preserve the original semantics.
Potentially reduce interpreter-level overhead.
[4]
C3.S2.R1
Repeated loop-bound calculation
A loop repeatedly evaluates a bound that is invariant.
Cache the bound before the loop.
The bound remains unchanged, and moving the evaluation is safe.
Avoid repeated calculations.
[2]
C3.S2.R2
Loop-invariant expression
An expression depends only on values that do not change during iteration.
Apply loop-invariant code motion.
No relevant side effects or changed exception behavior.
Reduce repeated computation from N evaluations to one where applicable.
[2]
C3.S2.R3
Repeated configuration lookup
The same configuration value or constant expression is retrieved in every iteration.
Store the stable result in a local variable outside the loop.
The underlying value cannot change during the loop.
Reduce repeated lookups and calculations.
[2], [4]
C3.S2.R4
Multiple passes over the same collection
Separate loops calculate independent results from the same data.
Combine compatible passes into a single traversal.
Results can be computed together without changing dependencies or behavior.
Reduce repeated traversal and collection access.
[7]
C3.S3.R1
Repeated regular-expression compilation
A fixed pattern is repeatedly prepared inside a loop.
Compile the pattern once and reuse it.
Pattern and flags remain constant.
Avoid repeated setup; benefit depends on runtime caching.
[5]
C3.S3.R2
Repeated container allocation
A temporary container is initialized every iteration but can safely be reused.
Move allocation outside the loop and reset or reuse the container.
No stale data or cross-iteration state leakage.
Reduce allocation and cleanup overhead.
[4], [6]
C3.S3.R3
Repeated helper creation
A function, callback, or closure is recreated inside the loop.
Move reusable definitions outside the loop.
Captured variables and per-iteration behavior remain equivalent.
Reduce repeated object creation where material.
[2]
C3.S3.R4
Repeated resource acquisition
Files, connections, or other costly resources are opened or initialized for each item.
Reuse resources or batch operations.
Resource lifetime, isolation, errors, and cleanup remain correct.
Reduce repeated setup and resource-management overhead.
[1], [7]
C3.S4.R1
Repeated membership scans
A nested loop checks membership in a list repeatedly.
Build a set or suitable index for repeated lookups.
Hashability, equality, duplicates, ordering, and memory are considered.
Often reduce expected lookup work from O(nm) to O(n+m).
[7]
C3.S4.R2
Repeated linear search by key
An inner loop searches a collection for a record or identifier.
Build a dictionary or map keyed by the lookup field.
Key uniqueness and duplicate-handling behavior are preserved.
Reduce repeated search work.
[7]
C3.S4.R3
Duplicate pair comparisons
A symmetric comparison evaluates both (i,j) and (j,i).
Iterate over unique pairs using an upper-triangular traversal.
Self-pairs and duplicate comparisons are unnecessary.
Approximately halve pair evaluations while retaining O(n2) complexity.
[7]
C3.S4.R4
Repeated nested joins or filters
Nested loops repeatedly match records across large datasets.
Consider hash joins, indexed lookups, database joins, or vectorized operations.
Join semantics, missing values, ordering, and memory are considered.
Potentially reduce repeated search and processing.
[7], [8]
C3.S5.R1
Search continues after a match
A loop finds the required item but keeps processing remaining elements.
Add break, early return, or an equivalent short-circuit search.
No later iteration is needed for required side effects or results.
Avoid unnecessary iterations.
[4]
C3.S5.R2
Full Boolean scan
All elements are checked to determine whether any item satisfies a condition.
Use short-circuiting any() or an equivalent operation.
Only the Boolean result is needed; predicate side effects are not relied upon.
Stop at the first successful match.
[4]
C3.S5.R3
Validation continues after failure
Validation checks all records although only the first failure is needed.
Exit immediately when the first invalid record is found.
The application does not require all validation errors.
Avoid processing remaining records.
[4]
C3.S5.R4
Missing conclusive termination
An aggregation continues after a provably final result has been reached.
Introduce a safe early-termination condition.
No remaining input can change the required answer.
Reduce unnecessary computation.
[7]
C3.S6.R1
Full processing for partial output
A transformation processes all elements, but only the first k outputs are consumed.
Use lazy iteration or bounded processing, such as islice().
Only k outputs are needed.
Avoid processing unused elements and reduce intermediate memory.
[6]
C3.S6.R2
Full sorting for an extreme value
The entire collection is sorted only to obtain its minimum or maximum.
Use min() or max().
No sorted sequence is required.
Replace typical O(nn) sorting with O(n) scanning.
[4], [7]
C3.S6.R3
Filtering after bulk retrieval
All records are fetched before filtering in application code.
Push filtering and projection to the database or data source.
The source supports equivalent filtering and required semantics are preserved.
Reduce transferred data and application-side processing.
[8]
C3.S6.R4
Full sorting for top-k results
All elements are sorted when only a small number of best results are required.
Use a bounded heap or suitable selection algorithm.
Ordering, tie-breaking, and top-k semantics are preserved.
Potentially reduce work to O(nk) for heap-based selection.
[7]
C3.S7.R1
Removing items during list iteration
A list is structurally modified while traversing it.
Use filtered-list construction or a safe in-place filtering strategy.
Preserve ordering, duplicates, object identity where required, and side effects.
Avoid skipped elements and repeated mutation overhead.
[4]
C3.S7.R2
Unsafe collection mutation
A Java collection is structurally modified outside the supported iterator mechanism.
Use Iterator.remove() or a suitable collection filtering operation.
The collection supports the operation.
Preserve defined iteration behavior and avoid modification errors.
[9]
C3.S7.R3
Repeated resizing during iteration
Repeated insertions or removals cause costly shifting or resizing.
Batch changes or separate traversal from mutation.
Deferred changes do not affect later decisions.
Reduce repeated structural modification overhead.
[7]
C3.S7.R4
Missing loop progress or termination
A while loop lacks a valid state update or exit condition.
Correct the progress update, loop invariant, or termination condition.
Termination matches the algorithm's intended behavior.
Prevent unbounded execution and wasted resources.
