Detection condition
Recommended optimization strategy
Preconditions / constraints
Expected benefit
Supporting reference
C4.S1-R01
Expensive condition first
An expensive predicate executes before a cheap guard.
Reorder predicates to evaluate cheap, selective conditions first.
Preserve Boolean semantics, side effects, and exceptions.
May reduce unnecessary predicate evaluations.
[1], [2], [6]
C4.S1-R02
Non-short-circuit Boolean operators
Boolean expressions use operators that evaluate both operands unnecessarily.
Use short-circuit logical operators where appropriate.
Verify language semantics and operand types.
May avoid evaluating the second operand.
[1], [2], [6]
C4.S1-R03
Poor predicate ordering
Independent conditions are evaluated in an inefficient order.
Order pure predicates by cost and short-circuit probability.
Reordering must preserve behavior.
May reduce average evaluation cost.
[1], [2], [6]
C4.S2-R01
Constant condition
A conditional expression is always true or false.
Simplify the condition and remove unreachable branches.
Verify runtime assumptions.
Eliminates unnecessary checks.
[1], [2], [5]
C4.S2-R02
Repeated condition evaluation
An unchanged, potentially expensive condition is evaluated repeatedly.
Compute the result once and reuse it.
Inputs and relevant state must remain unchanged.
May reduce repeated computation.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S2-R03
Redundant conditional checks
Conditions overlap or duplicate logic already handled by another branch.
Simplify the logic and remove redundant checks.
Preserve branch priority and edge cases.
May reduce condition evaluations.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S3-R01
Deep conditional nesting
Several nested branches obscure the main execution path.
Use guard clauses and early returns.
Preserve cleanup and return behavior.
Simplifies control flow and may reduce checks.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S3-R02
Repeated conditional dispatch
Similar long conditional structures select behavior repeatedly.
Consider polymorphism or a dispatch table.
Account for dispatch overhead and maintain equivalent behavior.
May simplify repeated logic.
[1], [3], [4], [7]
C4.S3-R03
Complex conditional expression
Long expressions repeat subconditions or complicate decisions.
Simplify conditions or extract reusable predicates.
Preserve evaluation order and side effects.
May reduce redundant work.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S4-R01
Missing else-if chain
Separate if statements test mutually exclusive cases.
Use an if/elif or if/else if chain.
Cases must be mutually exclusive or safely combined.
May skip unnecessary subsequent checks.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S4-R02
Repeated discriminator checks
Multiple branches compare the same variable against alternatives.
Consider switch, pattern matching, or dispatch tables.
Preserve matching and fall-through semantics.
May simplify branch selection.
[1], [3], [4], [7]
C4.S4-R03
Missing fallback handling
A required default case is absent, causing repeated downstream checks.
Add an appropriate else or default branch.
Preserve error handling and intended behavior.
May simplify fallback control flow.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S5-R01
Repeated type checks in loops
Object types are repeatedly checked inside a loop.
Move invariant checks outside the loop or group objects by type.
Types must remain stable; grouping may add overhead.
May reduce repeated type-checking work.
[1], [3], [4], [7]
C4.S5-R02
Type-based conditional dispatch
Long conditional chains choose behavior according to object type.
Consider polymorphism, interfaces, or visitor/strategy patterns.
Account for design complexity and dispatch overhead.
May reduce repeated type-based conditions.
[1], [3], [4], [7]
C4.S5-R03
Expensive type introspection
Reflection or string-based type comparisons occur frequently.
Use suitable direct type checks or established dispatch mechanisms.
Preserve inheritance and dynamic-type behavior.
May reduce inspection overhead.
[1], [3], [4], [7]
C4.S6-R01
Exceptions for expected lookup outcomes
Exceptions are frequently raised for routine missing-key or missing-value cases.
Use appropriate membership checks or default-value lookup APIs.
Avoid duplicate lookup work and concurrency issues.
May reduce exception-handling overhead.
[1], [2], [3], [4]
C4.S6-R02
Exceptions for routine validation
Expected invalid inputs are handled through frequent exceptions.
Validate common cases or use APIs with explicit failure results.
Do not hide unexpected failures.
May reduce exception overhead.
[1], [2], [3], [4]
C4.S6-R03
Exceptions for loop control
Exceptions repeatedly signal ordinary loop termination.
Use explicit loop conditions or sentinel values.
Preserve termination and error behavior.
May avoid repeated exception handling.
[1], [2], [3], [4]
C4.S7-R01
Missing empty-input fast path
Empty input triggers unnecessary setup or traversal.
Return early for empty input when valid.
Preserve required validation and side effects.
Avoids unnecessary processing.
[1], [2], [3], [6]
C4.S7-R02
Missing trivial-case fast path
Simple inputs use the full general-purpose algorithm.
Handle simple cases before invoking the general algorithm.
Preserve results and edge-case semantics.
May reduce work for trivial inputs.
[1], [2], [3], [6]
C4.S7-R03
Missing inexpensive pre-check
Expensive processing starts before a cheap check can reject the input.
Perform safe, inexpensive checks first.
Avoid duplicate work and incorrect rejection.
May skip unnecessary computation.
[1], [2], [3], [6]
C4.S8-R01
Non-idiomatic emptiness check
Python code uses len(items) == 0 for ordinary collection emptiness.
Prefer if not items when appropriate.
Confirm that truthiness represents emptiness.
Improves clarity; energy gains are unproven.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S8-R02
Unchained comparisons
Python repeats a middle comparison, such as a < b and b < c.
Consider a < b < c.
Ensure equivalent evaluation and side-effect behavior.
May avoid repeated evaluation of the middle expression.
[1], [2], [3]
C4.S8-R03
Incorrect floating-point comparison
Exact equality is used where tolerance is needed, or NaN is checked using ordinary equality.
Use justified numerical tolerances and suitable NaN checks.
Choose tolerances according to domain requirements.
Improves correctness; energy benefits are unproven.
