C5 — Suboptimal Data Structures: 


Rule ID
Root cause / pattern
Detection condition
Recommended optimization strategy
Preconditions / constraints
Expected benefit
Supporting reference
C5.S1.R1
Inefficient linear-search structure for frequent membership tests
IF a list or array is repeatedly searched for membership AND the collection is sufficiently large AND fast membership checking is the dominant operation, THEN flag inefficient data structure selection.
Replace linear search with hash-based lookup: Convert the collection into a hash set or hash table for repeated membership checks.
Elements must be hashable; ordering and duplicates must not be required for the lookup. The cost of constructing the hash structure must be justified by lookup frequency.
Average-case lookup complexity can improve from O(n) to O(1), reducing repeated comparisons and execution time.
https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/2674005.2674994
C5.S1.R2
Data representation does not match the dominant access pattern
IF records are stored in a row-oriented or nested structure AND the workload repeatedly processes only a small subset of fields across many records, THEN flag the representation as a candidate for optimization.
Use a column-oriented representation: Store frequently processed fields in contiguous arrays or columns rather than repeatedly traversing complete records.
Suitable for column-wise processing or analytical workloads. Record relationships, data types, and update requirements must be preserved.
Reduced unnecessary data access, improved memory locality, and potentially lower memory and CPU overhead.
https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/1376616.1376712
C5.S2.R1
Missing auxiliary lookup index
IF a collection is repeatedly scanned to locate records by a key AND the same collection is queried multiple times, THEN flag the absence of an efficient lookup structure.
Introduce a hash-based index: Construct a dictionary or hash map that maps each lookup key to its corresponding record.
Keys must be hashable and appropriately unique. Index creation and maintenance costs must be lower than the cost of repeated scans.
Reduces repeated linear searches and improves lookup performance, with additional memory required for the index.
T. H. Cormen, C. E. Leiserson, R. L. Rivest, and C. Stein, Introduction to Algorithms, 4th ed. Cambridge, MA, USA: MIT Press, 2022, ch. 11, “Hash Tables,” pp. 272–302. 
C5.S2.R2
Repeated computation or retrieval of frequently requested results
IF the same expensive lookup, query, or computation is repeatedly performed on unchanged data, THEN flag the absence of reusable precomputed results.
Introduce caching or materialization: Store frequently reused results in an auxiliary lookup structure instead of recomputing them.
Results must be reusable; cache invalidation or refresh must preserve correctness. Storage and maintenance costs must be justified.
Reduced repeated computations, fewer data accesses, and faster subsequent requests.
https://research.ibm.com/publications/dynamic-materialization-of-query-views-for-data-warehouse-workloads
C5.S3.R1
Over-provisioned record representation
IF a large collection of simple, fixed-schema records uses a general-purpose representation with unnecessary metadata or storage overhead, THEN flag the representation for optimization.
Use compact data representation: Replace heavyweight or general-purpose representations with compact arrays or structured storage appropriate to the workload.
Schema must be sufficiently stable. Required field access, updates, and semantics must be preserved. Measure memory and conversion costs before replacement.
Reduced memory footprint, potentially improved cache locality, and lower memory-access overhead.
https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/1376616.1376712
C5.S3.R2
Excessive storage overhead for approximate membership information
IF a large collection is maintained primarily for membership checks AND exact membership confirmation is unnecessary, THEN identify a compact probabilistic representation opportunity.
Use a compact membership filter: Replace an unnecessarily large membership representation with a suitable Bloom or Cuckoo filter.
False positives must be acceptable. Required deletion support, error rate, and memory constraints must be considered.
Reduced storage requirements and efficient membership testing for suitable workloads.
https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2674005.2674994
C5.S4.R1
Unnecessary intermediate collection materialization
IF an intermediate list or collection is created only to pass its elements to another operation AND it is not reused or otherwise required, THEN flag unnecessary materialization.
Eliminate intermediate structures: Use direct iteration, streaming, or fusion of producer and consumer operations instead of constructing temporary collections.
Intermediate results must not be needed for reuse, indexing, mutation, or multiple traversals. Preserve evaluation order and side effects.
Reduced temporary allocations, memory usage, and intermediate-processing overhead.
https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/165180.165214 

https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167642316300880?via%3Dihub
C5.S4.R2
Repeated serialization/deserialization or avoidable representation conversion
IF data is repeatedly serialized and deserialized within a performance-critical processing path without requiring an interchange format at every step, THEN flag redundant conversion overhead.
Avoid repeated representation changes: Retain a consistent internal representation or use direct/zero-copy access when supported.
Serialization required for persistence, communication, security boundaries, or interoperability must be preserved. Data ownership and lifetime must remain correct.
Reduced conversion overhead, fewer memory copies, and potentially faster data processing.
