C9 — Inefficient External Data Access 


Rule ID
Root cause / pattern
Detection condition
Recommended optimization strategy
Preconditions / constraints
Expected benefit
Supporting reference
C9.S1.R1
Fragmented database write operations
IF database records are repeatedly inserted individually inside a loop AND the database supports bulk operations, THEN flag fragmented database writes.
Batch database operations: Replace repeated individual INSERT operations with bulk insertion or batched writes.
Maintain data integrity, transaction boundaries, insertion ordering where required, and error handling. Choose a batch size appropriate for the database.
Fewer database operations, reduced I/O overhead, and potentially improved execution time and energy efficiency.
Research paper
C9.S1.R2
Excessive small file-write operations
IF a program repeatedly performs small file writes AND the writes can be safely accumulated, THEN flag fragmented file I/O.
Buffered I/O: Combine small writes using buffering and coordinated flushing rather than issuing unnecessary individual physical write operations.
Preserve data ordering, durability requirements, flush behavior, and memory constraints. Avoid buffering when immediate persistence is necessary.
Reduced storage I/O overhead, improved throughput, and potentially lower CPU and storage energy consumption.
Research paper
C9.S2.R1
Retrieving unnecessary database columns
IF a query retrieves all columns using SELECT * AND subsequent application logic consumes only a subset of those columns, THEN flag oversized retrieval.
Column projection: Retrieve only the required columns by specifying them explicitly in the query.
Required columns must be identifiable. Preserve attributes needed for filtering, joins, object mapping, and subsequent processing.
Reduced data transferred, memory utilization, and unnecessary I/O.
Research paper
C9.S2.R2
Retrieving an entire dataset when only a limited result is required
IF a query retrieves a complete result set AND the application subsequently selects only the first K records according to a defined ordering, THEN flag unnecessary result retrieval.
Server-side result limiting: Apply suitable top-K query processing or ORDER BY ... LIMIT instead of retrieving and filtering the entire dataset in application memory.
The required ordering and selection semantics must be preserved. The database must support the requested result-limiting operation.
Reduced result transfer and application memory usage; potentially lower database processing cost.
Research paper
C9.S3.R1
N+1 database query anti-pattern
IF a query retrieves N records AND a loop performs an additional query to retrieve related information for each record, THEN flag repeated dependent retrieval.
Eliminate N+1 queries: Replace repeated dependent queries with a combined JOIN, appropriate ORM eager loading, or batched prefetching.
Preserve relationship cardinality, filtering behavior, and correctness. Avoid excessive eager loading that increases memory consumption or duplicates large result sets.
Fewer database round-trips, lower query overhead, and improved application response time.
Research paper
C9.S3.R2
Repeated ORM queries caused by unnecessary lazy retrieval
IF application code repeatedly accesses related database entities through ORM calls AND this triggers multiple avoidable retrieval operations, THEN flag an inefficient retrieval path.
Optimize ORM retrieval: Refactor repeated ORM access into a combined database retrieval operation or appropriately batched relationship loading.
Required associations must be known. Preserve query semantics, lazy-loading requirements, and memory constraints.
Reduced query count, fewer database interactions, and potentially faster execution.
Research paper
C9.S4.R1
Unnecessary Cartesian products during query execution
IF a multi-table query creates a Cartesian product because required join relationships are missing or an inefficient join structure is used, THEN flag a potentially expensive query pattern.
Optimize join structure: Introduce the intended join predicates and use an appropriate join ordering or equivalent relational transformation.
The Cartesian product must not be intentional. Preserve relational semantics and verify the resulting execution plan.
Avoids unnecessary intermediate combinations and may substantially reduce query execution cost.
Research paper
C9.S4.R2
Unnecessary duplicate elimination in SQL queries
IF a query uses UNION AND the component queries are proven to produce mutually disjoint results with no duplicates requiring removal, THEN flag unnecessary duplicate elimination.
Replace UNION with UNION ALL: Eliminate unnecessary duplicate-removal processing when the resulting data is guaranteed to remain equivalent.
Must prove duplicate elimination is unnecessary, including duplicates within individual query results. Preserve result semantics.
Avoids unnecessary duplicate-removal work and can reduce query execution time.
Research paper
C9.S4.R3
Repeated query optimization for similar parameterized queries
IF structurally identical parameterized queries are repeatedly executed AND the DBMS repeatedly incurs significant query-planning overhead, THEN identify an opportunity for plan reuse.
Parameterized query-plan caching: Use supported prepared-query or cached-plan mechanisms to reuse suitable execution plans where beneficial.
Database must support applicable caching mechanisms. Parameter sensitivity and plan validity must be considered; cached plans are not always optimal.
Reduced repeated planning overhead and potentially faster query execution.
Research paper







